-- title:
--   Counit idempotent cuts out a Hopf quotient over a local ring
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local ring and let $H$ be a commutative $R$-algebra carrying a Hopf $R$-algebra structure and finite as an $R$-module. Let $e_0 \in H$ be an idempotent element whose counit value is $\varepsilon(e_0) = 1$, and suppose that the quotient ring $H/(1-e_0)$ by the ideal generated by $1 - e_0$ is a local ring. The conclusion asserts the existence of a type $H_0$ (in the same universe as $H$) equipped with a commutative ring structure and a Hopf $R$-algebra structure, together with a bialgebra homomorphism $\pi : H \to H_0$ over $R$, such that: $\pi$ is surjective; the kernel of $\pi$, viewed as an $R$-algebra map, is exactly the ideal $\mathrm{span}\,\{1 - e_0\}$; $H_0$ is a local ring; $H_0$ is finite as an $R$-module; if the comultiplication of $H$ is cocommutative then so is that of $H_0$; and if $H$ is flat as an $R$-module then so is $H_0$. Thus $(1-e_0)$ is a Hopf ideal and the quotient inherits locality, module-finiteness, cocommutativity and flatness.
--
--   In scheme language this says that for a finite commutative group scheme $G = \operatorname{Spec} H$ over a local ring, the closed subscheme $\operatorname{Spec}\bigl(H/(1-e_0)\bigr)$ cut out by the counit idempotent $e_0$ is a closed subgroup scheme, the connected component of the identity when $e_0$ is the idempotent of the local factor through which $\varepsilon$ factors. It is used in the analysis of finite flat group schemes over local rings, in particular to produce the identity component of a group scheme whose Cartier dual is local at $p$, and in the construction of Néron-type extensions of points on modular curves.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_HopfAlgebra_exists_bialgHom_surjective_ker_eq_span_one_sub_of_counit_eq_one_of_isLocalRing_quotient.lean

